 able to earn more money that  way  and i really think it comes down to your  definition of fair which is kind of  hard to measure in an objective manner  so a lot of the problems that we find is  like just too few items even have the  sustainability information  and then as i mentioned like not all  labels are standardized and they can  mean different things  for clothing there's very many different  tech summaries out there so it's not  very easy to map and put into one  database  and to be able to say like this product  ranks above this one  and also a lot of these data sources  require a lot of cleaning  we're not an incredibly big company and  our team alone is only i think four or  five people  with two people very dedicated to  working on this um so it's really too  much work for us  alone  so we have a partnership also with some  domain experts from the bht which is the  berliner hoshula for technic and the  technician tu which is the technical  university  and we're working on this project called  the green consumption assistant so i  really recommend checking this out if  this is interesting to you  this website is completely in german um  currently i i'm not sure if it's going  to be translated into english  but it explains there what we're trying  to do and it's a five-year project to  try and create solutions like the ones  i'm talking about  and one of those solutions is the green  database so this is an open data set  um and it's currently what fuels the  occasional shopping vertical which is  what i showed you earlier you can check  it out on github it's still in progress  so please don't go there and  expect it to be like this super shiny  completely finished thing we're working  on it a lot but it's still a work in  progress  and i think this is really interesting  because this is really our first  technique into thinking about how we can  build a data source  to really be able to rank and put and  boost these kind of results into our  search results  but we need to go further than that  so it's okay if we have a data bank but  that will of course as you probably know  if you work with data require a lot of  updating  especially fashion goes out of season  really really quick  so we really have to ensure that things  are moving quickly and automated because  of this  but one of our goals is actually to add  these sustainability labels as a  standard to the schemas  for the products  that are on this schema.org  and i think this is a really good  approach and this is something we can  maybe all think about and maybe those  things you also know in your own work  where you can actually build this into  the standards so like  another idea i've heard about would be  to have a co  like a carbon.txt like a robot.txt on  websites  that lists some of the things that that  website is trying to do for the climate  these are small solutions but these are  things that kind of make sure  it's built in from the start and then we  have the data and we can go and build  products on top of it  so  i promised try end more positive and  like what can we do i think things that  you can do are support advocating for  sustainable software where you work  so you can think about what that means  there's lots of great websites like  climate.tech is a great resource for  finding out about how you can make your  infra more sustainable how you can do  more sustainable web dev  you'll also find jobs on there if you're  interested in moving into the sector  but maybe you can also apply small  things in the company where you're  working so instead of using a glossy  paper in the printer you can use  recycled paper or recycled toilet paper  for example  you can switch to a closure so there's a  sales pitch  and if you're really really stuck  because i know that this is often a  problem you can use hashtag g at the end  and it will take you to google  you can follow the gca project which is  what i mentioned and support us to make  these standards better and more  transparent and i really welcome  everyone to go check out  follow the project on github and like i  said it's not complete but  you can follow it and see what you can  do to maybe get involved as well  and we can't do it alone so i read these  results on stack overflow while i was on  the train here from berlin and i have to  be honest i was really really upset  and disappointed that still in 2022  uh we just like i mean you can't barely  read this graphic and i apologize but  it's um split by gender  of people that work as technologists  and it's 91.88  men  and then  a breakdown of like women and non-binary  um preferring not to say and other  we also see that it's predominantly in  the northern hemisphere where the  biggest impacts of climate change are  not yet seen  that are also working in the sector so i  think it's really really important  that we start involving more people in  this conversation as well  we have a responsibility because we're  the biggest groups in this but we also  need to make sure that we're looking for  the other voices to support us with this  work  so  thank you very much first of all for  listening i hope that this sparked some  interest and maybe you want to talk  about it over a burger or i'm not sure  what is on the barbecue today some veg  everything  uh vegan options  but if you do have questions you want to  reach out to me and connect you can  reach out to me on linkedin you can  write me on twitter i would love to hear  from you i would love to hear your  thoughts on this so thank you very much  you
 

